# The tower that fell in (Dungeon Expansion Plan V1; Elven Tower, chapter 11: three firings of the door's ward bring the
# tower down, and what it held waits under the stone, hours of digging). The island's map can't change, so the black wall stays
# standing round a pit and the party digs from the doorway (prop vrt_rubble_heap, and the door's own conversation once the
# door is gone). Three good shifts (Athletics DC 14, two hours each) reach the lantern room's strongbox and Van Richten's
# loose pages, so nothing the story needs is lost: the Tarokka place, the casebook's flags, the loot.

~ heap
Narrator: Inside the standing shell, the rooms have dropped straight down into a pit like nested boxes. Whatever the hunter kept up there is still buried at the bottom, waiting for a shovel.
if flag.vrt_strongbox_dug
-> END
endif
-> heap_menu

~ heap_menu
* Start hauling stone and dig into the rubble. -> dig
* Leave the heap of stone as it is. -> END

~ dig
time +120
check Athletics DC 14 -> dig_ok | dig_fail

~ dig_ok
set vrt_dig += 1
if flag.vrt_dig >= 3
-> dig_found
endif
Narrator: Two hours of levering timber and passing heavy black stone from hand to hand. Your shoulders ache and your palms are raw, but the pit is deeper.
-> heap_menu

~ dig_fail
Narrator: Every heavy block you pry free brings three more sliding down to take its place. Two hours pass in the cold damp, and the hole is no deeper.
-> heap_menu

~ dig_found
set vrt_strongbox_dug
Narrator: A beam shifts, opening a dark pocket under the old window sill. Inside, an iron strongbox rests unharmed on its side, surrounded by dry pages from the hunter's desk.
reading give van_richtens_tower
if not flag.van_richten_strongbox_opened
set van_richten_strongbox_opened
gold +120
give holy_water 3
give potion_of_healing_greater 2
give spell_scroll_level_1
give ammunition_of_slaying_undead__arrow 2
endif
-> END

~ notes
set van_richten_notes_read
set van_richten_identity_known
set den_way_known
Narrator: Loose sheets from a casebook, a neat ledger, and a map of the valley floor. The fallen stone kept the lake mist off them; every cramped line remains legible.
-> END
